### In-Depth Study: Java Generics, Bounded Types, Autoboxing & Type Erasure

#### 1. Why Generics are Essential:
Before Generics (pre-JDK 1.5), collections stored raw `Object` references. Developers were forced to write explicit type casts (e.g., `(String) list.get(0)`), creating severe risks of runtime `ClassCastException`. Generics shifts type checking entirely to **compile time**.

#### 2. Type Erasure:
The Java compiler implements Generics using **Type Erasure**:
- At compile-time, type parameters are validated and replaced with their upper bounds (or `Object` if unbounded).
- Appropriate bytecode casts are inserted automatically.
- Generic type parameters do not exist at runtime, preserving backward compatibility with legacy bytecodes.

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation: Generic Class & Generic Method:
```java
import java.util.*;

// 1. Generic Class with Type Parameter <T>
class StorageBox<T> {
    private T item;

    public void setItem(T item) { this.item = item; }
    public T getItem() { return item; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "StorageBox contains: " + item + " [Type: " + item.getClass().getSimpleName() + "]";
    }
}

public class GenericsAutoboxingDemo {

    // 2. Generic Method that prints elements of any array
    public static <E> void displayArray(E[] elements) {
        System.out.print("[Generic Method Display]: ");
        for (E element : elements) {
            System.out.print(element + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 3. Autoboxing & Unboxing
        List<Integer> numbers = new ArrayList<>();
        numbers.add(100); // Autoboxing: int 100 -> Integer.valueOf(100)
        int primitiveVal = numbers.get(0); // Unboxing: numbers.get(0).intValue()
        System.out.println("Autoboxed & Unboxed value: " + primitiveVal);

        // 4. Testing Generic Class with different types
        StorageBox<String> stringBox = new StorageBox<>();
        stringBox.setItem("Core Java Textbook");
        System.out.println(stringBox);

        StorageBox<Integer> intBox = new StorageBox<>();
        intBox.setItem(999);
        System.out.println(intBox);

        // 5. Testing Generic Method
        String[] languages = { "Java", "Python", "C++", "Rust" };
        Integer[] primes = { 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 };

        displayArray(languages);
        displayArray(primes);
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Generics guarantees compile-time type invariance without runtime performance penalties.}$$
